# -*- mode:org;coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: vl-file-systime: element count and day-of-week convention differ from BricsCAD/Autodesk
#+AUTHOR: Pascal J. Bourguignon
#+DATE: 2026-08-09
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: divergence
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-vl-file-systime-parity

Found by the alpm verification probes (alpm repo,
tests/verification/verification.lsp, probe V-2) run at the same
moment (Sun 2026-08-09 00:05 local) on both engines:

- clautolisp 1.8.21:      (2026 8 0 9 0 5 28)      -- 7 elements
- BricsCAD V26 (macOS):   (2026 8 7 9 0 5 55 0)    -- 8 elements

* Differences

1. Element count: BricsCAD returns the Autodesk-documented 8-element
   list (year month day-of-week day hours minutes seconds
   MILLISECONDS); clautolisp omits the milliseconds element.
   Autodesk reference example: (2003 9 4 10 16 25 4 230).
2. Day-of-week convention for the same Sunday:
   - clautolisp: 0  (C struct tm convention, Sunday = 0; consistent
     with an earlier probe: Friday 2026-08-07 -> 5)
   - BricsCAD V25/V26: 7
   - AutoCAD 2022 RUNTIME (probed 2026-08-09, alpm CI job
     verify:autocad:windows, job 15795067578): (2026 8 0 9 12 21 8 1)
     -- Sunday = 0, WITH milliseconds. So AutoCAD's runtime uses
     Sunday=0, contradicting Autodesk's own documentation example
     (2003-09-10 Wednesday documented as day-of-week 4, implying
     Sunday=1); and BricsCAD (Sunday=7) diverges from AutoCAD.
   clautolisp therefore already matches AutoCAD's day-of-week
   convention; only the milliseconds element is missing for
   AutoCAD-shape parity. For bricscad-* dialects, Sunday=7 would be
   the parity target.

* Suggested fix

- Add the milliseconds element (0 when the platform cannot provide
  it) so the shape matches the vendors.
- Match the day-of-week convention of the emulated dialect
  (autocad-* vs bricscad-*), after confirming AutoCAD's actual
  runtime value vs its documentation.
- Extend the conformance suite with a fixed-file check of the shape.

* Source

alpm CI job verify:bricscad:macos (pipeline 2744343498, job
15791433487) and verify:clautolisp:macos (job 15791433486),
2026-08-09.

* Triage 2026-08-09 — code located; one open decision

** Where it lives

=clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp=:

- =builtin-vl-file-systime= (l.2416) builds the 7-element list
  =(year month dow day hour minute second)= from =decode-universal-time=
  of =(file-write-date resolved)=;
- =autolisp-day-of-week= (l.2410) is =(mod (1+ cl-dow) 7)=, i.e. CL's
  Monday=0..Sunday=6 remapped to Sunday=0..Saturday=6 — which is
  exactly the Sunday=0 the probe observed, and exactly AutoCAD 2022's
  runtime convention.

** Split into two independent parts

*** Part A — element count (unambiguous, do it now)
Both vendors return 8 elements; clautolisp returns 7. This is a plain
shape bug with no divergence question attached: append the
milliseconds element. Note the substrate limit — =file-write-date= is
defined to second granularity, so the value will be a constant 0
unless the implementation-specific stat is used (SBCL
=sb-posix:stat-mtime= / CCL =ccl::%stat=, both nanosecond-capable but
non-portable, and the AGENTS "isolate implementation-specific code
behind a narrow interface" rule applies). The issue itself already
licenses "0 when the platform cannot provide it", so 0 is an
acceptable first cut; record it as a =;;; SPEC-UNCERTAIN:= marker if
it ships that way.

*** Part B — day-of-week (needs pjb's ruling)
Three conventions are in evidence and the vendor documentation
contradicts its own runtime:

| source                  | Sunday | note                          |
|-------------------------+--------+-------------------------------|
| AutoCAD 2022 runtime    |      0 | probed                        |
| Autodesk documentation  |      1 | implied by the 2003-09-10 ex. |
| BricsCAD V25/V26        |      7 | probed                        |
| clautolisp (today)      |      0 | matches AutoCAD's runtime     |

Per AGENTS "Dialect Divergence and Warnings" this is a *case 3*
(unresolved, symmetric) divergence on the face of it — no vendor is
obviously right, and a majority does not make one right — which would
mean every dialect except =lax= warns.

But =autolisp-spec-alpm-verification-findings.issue= carries a later
correction from pjb: "There's no reason to make a clautolisp specific
extension here. Let's match what bricscad does, and document it." Read
literally that selects Sunday=7 as the clautolisp behaviour, which
would *move clautolisp away from* the AutoCAD runtime it currently
matches, and it is in tension with this issue's own text ("clautolisp
therefore already matches AutoCAD's day-of-week convention").

So the ruling to confirm before writing Part B is one of:

1. per-dialect: =autocad-*= → Sunday=0, =bricscad-*= → Sunday=7,
   =clautolisp=/=strict= → pick one and warn (case-2 treatment);
2. uniformly Sunday=7 (the literal reading of the correction);
3. leave Sunday=0 and only document the divergence (status quo + spec).

Part A is not blocked by this and can ship first.

** Acceptance criteria
- vl-file-systime returns 8 elements on every dialect.
- A fixed-file conformance check pins the shape (issue's own third
  bullet) — the corpus has no shape assertion today.
- The chosen day-of-week rule is documented in the spec entry's
  =*** clautolisp= note per the deviation convention.

* RESOLVED 2026-08-09 (clautolisp 1.8.23, autolisp-spec 0.9.3)

** A premise of this issue was wrong: there is no third convention

Per pjb's instruction the reference documentation was scanned across
versions and vendors first. That scan overturns the framing above.

The example cited in this issue as Autodesk's,
=(2003 9 4 10 16 25 4 230)= "implying Sunday=1", does not appear on any
Autodesk page found, and it is internally inconsistent: 2003-09-10 is a
*Wednesday*, which is 3 under both candidate conventions, not 4. The
actual Autodesk example is =(1998 4 3 8 10 6 52)= — 1998-04-08, a
Wednesday, = 3. So the "Sunday=1" reading rested on a bad datum and
there is no third convention.

What the pages actually say:

| Reference page     | Elements documented | Example      | Weekday   | Value |
|--------------------+---------------------+--------------+-----------+-------|
| Autodesk (2016 +)  |                   7 | 1998-04-08   | Wednesday |     3 |
| Bricsys V25/CurVer |     7 (sample is 8) | 2019-10-24   | Thursday  |     4 |
| progeCAD           |                   7 | 2011-02-25   | Friday    |     5 |

Autodesk states "Monday is day 1 of day of week, Tuesday is day 2, and
so on" and stops; Bricsys states no numbering at all. **No documented
example falls on a Sunday.** Monday..Saturday are therefore 1..6
everywhere — that is the whole of what the documentation establishes —
and the divergence is *exactly one value wide*: the two engines fill the
documented silence differently (AutoCAD 2022 runtime Sunday=0, the C
=struct tm= convention; BricsCAD V25/V26 Sunday=7, ISO-8601). Neither
engine contradicts its own documentation.

That also dissolves the tension recorded in the Triage section: matching
BricsCAD does not require moving off AutoCAD except on Sundays, and the
POSIX completion pjb chose for the =clautolisp= dialect *is* AutoCAD's.

Both vendors' pages are wrong about the arity, in the same direction:
every probed engine returns a trailing milliseconds element, and the
Bricsys page contradicts itself within one screen — a seven-element
format line above an eight-value sample return =(2019 10 4 24 4 1 10 0)=.

** What shipped

- =builtin-vl-file-systime= returns eight elements in every dialect.
- =autolisp-day-of-week= takes the dialect: Monday..Saturday = 1..6
  everywhere; Sunday = 7 under =bricscad-v25= / =bricscad-v26=, 0
  (POSIX) everywhere else, including =clautolisp= and =strict=.
- =%file-write-date-milliseconds= is a narrow seam returning 0, with a
  =;;; SPEC-UNCERTAIN:= marker. =CL:FILE-WRITE-DATE= is defined in whole
  seconds; the filesystems underneath (APFS, ext4, NTFS) do keep a finer
  timestamp, so the seam is where an implementation-specific stat would
  go. pjb licensed 0 for now.
- Tests: =vl-file-systime-day-of-week-differs-only-on-sunday= exercises
  all seven weekdays across all eight dialect names. It tests the
  mapping directly rather than through a real file, because a file
  created while the suite runs lands on whatever weekday that is and
  could never exercise Sunday — the only value that differs. The three
  places that pinned the seven-element shape were updated
  (builtin-tests, the file-compat api-test, and the declarative
  scenario =mutations/file-systime-basic.sexp=).
- autolisp-spec entry rewritten with the per-vendor table, the
  documented-silence analysis and a =*** clautolisp= deviation note;
  Availability moved from "presumed compatible" to verified on both
  vendors. Spec document version 0.9.2 -> 0.9.3.
- Suite green: 6230 checks, 0 failures (SBCL).

** Residual work (not blocking, belongs to the vendor-probe register)

pjb: "then we'll verify bricscad and autocad implementation in the
various versions we may try it". The Sunday value has been probed on
AutoCAD 2022, BricsCAD V25 (Windows) and V26 (macOS) only, and only on
one Sunday. Probing further versions needs a run scheduled on a Sunday
or a file whose mtime is forced to one — the latter is the cheap way and
is worth adding to the alpm verification probe (set the mtime, then
read it back). Track under
=vendor-probe-autocad-bricscad-divergences.issue=.

** Addendum 2026-08-09 (pjb): the portable reading is =(mod dow 7)=

Because the divergence is confined to Sunday and the two values it takes
are 0 and 7, a single operation normalises every engine and every
dialect: =(mod day-of-week 7)=. =(mod 0 7)= and =(mod 7 7)= are both 0,
and Monday..Saturday (1..6) pass through =mod 7= unchanged, so the
result is always the POSIX =struct tm= numbering with no test for which
engine is running.

This supersedes the earlier advice to avoid position 3: portable code
should *read* it, through =mod 7=. Recorded in the spec entry (new
"Reading the day of week portably" subsection + a line in the
=*** clautolisp= note), in the user manual, and in RELEASE_NOTES; and
asserted by the regression test over all 7 weekdays x 8 dialects so the
identity cannot silently rot. Spec document version 0.9.3 -> 0.9.4.
